Ctrl/Cmd+W on a pinned tab selects the first unpinned tab in Ladybird,
rather than closing the pinned one, and only when the shortcut came
from the keyboard. Firefox does the same in closeTabOrWindow()
(browser-commands.js). Chromium instead shows a ToastId::kClosePinnedTab
confirmation toast first, and closes the tab on a second press.

Middle-click closes a pinned tab in Ladybird, as it does in Chromium’s
Tab::OnMouseReleased() and in the auxclick handler in Firefox’s tabs.js.
Close Tabs to Left/Right and Close Other Tabs skip pinned tabs in
Ladybird, as Chromium’s TabStripModel::GetIndicesClosedByCommand()
and Firefox’s _getTabsToTheStartFrom(), _getTabsToTheEndFrom(),
and removeAllTabsBut() do. Duplicate Tab gives an unpinned copy in
Ladybird, as Firefox’s duplicateTab() does (tabState.pinned = false);
Chromium’s DuplicateTabAt() pins the copy. And a drag never changes
a tab’s pin state in Ladybird, while Firefox’s adoptTab() pins an
adopted tab by its drop index.
